:name: Even Realities G1 lens
:description: One lens of a G1 pair: an nRF5340 with the board peripherals, running the firmware.
#
# Turns the current machine into a lens. Include it after g1.resc (which
# compiles the peripheral models and creates the radio medium "wireless"),
# once for each lens of the pair, straight after "mach create" and after
# setting that lens's inputs:
#
#   $app_bin  app-core image INCLUDING its 0x200-byte MCUboot header
#             (app_update.bin as shipped). Loaded at 0xC000, the MCUboot
#             primary slot, so the vector table lands at 0xC200.
#   $net_bin  net-core image WITHOUT its MCUboot header (vector table first;
#             net_core_app_update.bin with the first 0x200 bytes removed).
#             It is linked for 0x01008800, the slot behind the net-core
#             bootloader.
#   $slave    true for the slave lens of the pair; the master lens if unset.
#   $extstore the file that holds this lens's external flash, a raw image of
#             the 32 MiB chip. It is created if it does not exist, and
#             everything the firmware writes goes into it at once, so the
#             lens finds its flash as it left it the next time.
#   $deviceaddr0, $deviceaddr1
#             the lens's Bluetooth address as the two FICR DEVICEADDR words:
#             the low four bytes, and 0xFFFF0000 + the high two. The two
#             lenses of a pair need different ones. If unset, the master
#             lens's E9:A4:9E:29:70:D9.
#
# Set them after "mach create": a variable set while a machine is selected
# belongs to that machine, so each lens keeps its own images and role. The
# reset macro below uses them again whenever the lens reboots.
#
# No bootloader is emulated: each core's SP/PC are taken straight from its
# image's vector table, which is what MCUboot / b0n do after validation.

using sysbus
path add $ORIGIN

$slave?=false
$deviceaddr0?=0x9E2970D9
$deviceaddr1?=0xFFFFE9A4

# Run the two cores serially within each time quantum. In parallel, a
# peripheral driven by one core that raises the other core's NVIC line
# mid-quantum (IPC does this constantly) races the exception-entry handshake
# and the target core vectors into garbage. Serial execution removes the race
# and makes dual-core runs deterministic.
python "from Antmicro.Renode.Core import EmulationManager; EmulationManager.Instance.CurrentEmulation.MasterTimeSource.ExecuteInSerial = True"

machine LoadPlatformDescription @platform/nrf5340.repl
connector Connect sysbus.radio wireless

# Both cores run from the 64 MHz clock. Renode's default of 100 MIPS makes the
# firmware's calibrated busy-wait loops finish early; the UART driver's
# per-character wait then gives up before a character has left the line and
# sleeps a millisecond instead, which slows the boot log down tenfold.
sysbus.cpuapp PerformanceInMips 64
sysbus.cpunet PerformanceInMips 64

# JBD panel protocol as the G1 firmware speaks it: the panel ID is read on
# received bytes 2/3 (one dummy byte after the 0x9F opcode), and the
# pixel-window coordinate is sent big-endian.
spim_a.jbd IdReadLatency 1
spim_a.jbd CoordBigEndian true

# A full battery. 4500 mV is above the cell's 4.4 V on purpose: the firmware
# takes a state of charge that stays below 100% without changing for two
# minutes as a stuck fuel gauge and stops feeding the PMIC watchdog, and the
# modelled battery never changes.
sysbus.twim1.npm1300 BatteryVoltageMilliVolts 4500
sysbus.twim1.npm1300 BatteryCurrentMilliAmps 0

# An expired PMIC watchdog power-cycles the lens, as on hardware. This is how
# v1.66 firmware reboots itself: it stops feeding the watchdog and shortens
# its timeout to 100 ms.
sysbus.twim1.npm1300 ResetOnWatchdogExpiry true

# Lying flat on a table: 1 g on the IMU's Z axis. (The model's default is no
# gravity at all, which leaves the firmware's attitude filter drifting.) This
# is also where the IMU model's "don" gesture starts.
sysbus.twim2.lsm6dso AccelerationZ 1

# FICR/UICR: nRF5340 reset values from the Nordic SVD, with a fixed device id.
sysbus LoadBinary @data/ficr_app.bin 0x00FF0000
sysbus LoadBinary @data/uicr_app.bin 0x00FF8000
sysbus LoadBinary @data/ficr_net.bin 0x01FF0000
sysbus LoadBinary @data/uicr_net.bin 0x01FF8000

# The lens's Bluetooth address, in the net core's FICR (DEVICEADDR[0..1]).
# The firmware takes its MAC from there; the last three bytes end the name it
# advertises under.
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x01FF02A4 $deviceaddr0
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x01FF02A8 $deviceaddr1

# The external QSPI NOR, as it comes if $extstore does not exist yet: erased,
# apart from the settings area (system settings at chip offset 0x130000,
# whitelist 0x131000, user settings 0x134000, app settings 0x13E000), which
# is filled in as on a lens that has been through the factory, is paired with
# a second lens on ESB channel 34 and bound to a phone. Generated by
# g1_seed.py.
sysbus.qspi.qspi_flash LoadSegment 0x130000 @data/g1_extstore_seed.bin

# From here on the flash is the file: an existing $extstore replaces the
# contents above, a missing one is created from them.
sysbus.qspi.qspi_flash PersistTo $extstore

# Load both images and point each core at its vector table. Renode runs the
# macro named "reset" after every machine reset, so a firmware-initiated
# reboot restarts the firmware instead of jumping to the (absent) bootloader.
macro reset
"""
    # The role strap, P0.26: low = master lens, high = slave lens.
    sysbus.gpio0.strap_p0_26 Set $slave
    sysbus LoadBinary $app_bin 0x0000C000
    cpuapp VectorTableOffset 0x0000C200
    cpuapp SP `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x0000C200`
    cpuapp PC `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x0000C204`
    # The net image must sit at 0x01008800 for its absolute pointers to
    # resolve, but on release the net core fetches SP/PC from the flash base
    # 0x01000000 (where the net bootloader would be), so it is mirrored there.
    # The base copy goes first: it overlaps 0x01008800 and would clobber it.
    sysbus LoadBinary $net_bin 0x01000000
    sysbus LoadBinary $net_bin 0x01008800
    # Held in reset until the app core clears RESET.NETWORK.FORCEOFF.
    cpunet IsHalted true
    cpunet VectorTableOffset 0x01008800
    cpunet SP `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x01008800`
    cpunet PC `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x01008804`
"""
runMacro $reset
